From 6c1cce06bfbf8c15958d1308ee52b6f6c11c4acf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 19:52:59 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] warden: surface threat-set occupancy, and stop a slow custom
 feed eating the window

Two long-standing residual ceilings, both confirmed still open before touching
anything.

OCCUPANCY. threat_n was already in the stats JSON and no page ever read it
(RWDN_89 "Prefixes loaded" is the GEO count, not the threat set), so a user
piling on custom feeds had no way to see how close rw_threat was to maxelem -
and ipset silently drops entries past it. The ceiling was a bare 524288 repeated
at six ipset-create sites, so there was no single number to quote either. Added
RW_THREAT_MAXELEM/_S, replaced those six sites (the two further mentions are
prose in comments and stay English), and the stats JSON now emits threat_max
from that same define, so the page cannot drift from what the sets are actually
built with. Reaper_Warden.asp renders RWDN_100 "Threat entries: N / 524,288" in
the existing breakdown list, beside the geo prefix count.

FETCH BUDGET. One $CURL served curated feeds, user feeds and the ipdeny geo
fetches alike. Eight custom feeds at --retry 2 --max-time 40 is 8x3x40s = up to
16 minutes of one update window spent on hosts that may simply be gone. New
CURLC (--retry 1 --max-time 15) is used at the two CUSTOM fetch sites only -
curated and geo keep $CURL, because those are known-good hosts worth waiting on.
Same eight now cap at 8x2x15s = 4 minutes. CURLC gets the same
--proto/--proto-redir =https upgrade when curl supports it, so the redirect pin
is not weakened, and a timeout still logs "custom feed fetch FAILED" with the
previous set kept (swap-if-nonempty): a slow feed degrades to "no refresh",
never to "no data".

i18n: RWDN_100 appended to all 25 dicts in lockstep. RWDN_92-99 are deliberately
SKIPPED - reserved by an unlanded port-forward-exemption change; reusing them
would silently relabel its strings. Numbering gaps cost nothing, LnxDictPrep
re-indexes named tokens at build time.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
